# Phase 164.7 plan 04 — NEUTER LOG

**Every assertion this plan added must be able to FAIL.** Six neuters, three per
gate file, each one observed CAUGHT by the machine that is supposed to catch it,
each restored from a BYTE BACKUP and verified by sha256.

Measured 2026-09-07 on this worktree at the tree committed by this plan's first
three commits, on a throwaway pg-lane cluster
(`PGBIN=/opt/homebrew/opt/postgresql@16/bin`; the PATH `pg_ctl` is PostgreSQL 18,
which has no pg_cron, and the lane correctly refuses to boot on it).

## Restore protocol

⛔ Restore is by BYTE BACKUP and `cp`, never by a git working-tree restore
command. A restore-to-HEAD in a neuter harness silently destroys the
uncommitted work the harness itself is standing on; this harness copies the file
aside first, mutates in place, and copies it back, then compares sha256 before
and after. Six pairs, twelve hashes, all equal, and `git status --porcelain` is
empty at the end of every block.

## What each neuter defeats, and which layer catches it

| # | Neuter | Layer that must catch it | Observed |
|---|---|---|---|
| N1 | arm K's two `TEST FAILED (K)` raises are downgraded from `RAISE EXCEPTION` to `RAISE NOTICE` — the arm can no longer fail | the mutation runner's `--file … --arm K` lane | `SYNTHESISED(K)`, defect `synthesised-identity`, **biting: 0**. A NOTICE-carried identity is not ATTRIBUTABLE (GRAMMAR rule 3c: severity must be ERROR), so the arm is not counted as biting — the control refusing to accept a raise the gate did not really make |
| N2 | arm L's `RED-UNDER-M` twin line is deleted; the human `RED-UNDER` prose stays | `--parse-only`, the parity gate | `17 prose / 16 twin(s)`, static defect `parity`. The prose-only backfill this phase exists to prevent is a hard failure, not a silent omission |
| N3 | the precondition that refuses a COMMITTED-OPEN activation row — three legs, see below | the lane, three ways | see below |

## N3 is three legs, and it corrected a claim rather than confirming one

The plan asked for a neuter proving the precondition "guards arm A's meaning",
i.e. that arm A would go RED for the wrong reason without it. **It does not, and
this is the measurement that says so.**

* **leg (a) — precondition INTACT, a committed-TRUE row seeded by a `sql` step on
  arm A's twin.** The gate exits 3 having emitted **no** `TEST FAILED (…)` at
  all; the runner reports `NO-IDENTITY` / `wrong-first-failure` with
  `SIGHTINGS: none`. The guard FIRES, and `TEST ABORTED` is deliberately not an
  identity the runner can attribute — exactly as the comment above it claims.
* **leg (b) — the same seeded row, precondition downgraded to a NOTICE.** Arm A's
  own twin now reddens arm A cleanly: `RED (identity ok)`, biting 1. So the
  precondition is the ONLY thing standing between the two outcomes.
* **leg (c) — the same seeded row, precondition a NOTICE, and NO mutation at
  all.** The lane exits 0 and the file prints
  `ALL 12 ARMS EXECUTED (A-L) and passed`. **This is the leg that settles it:** a
  committed-open switch does not make any arm vacuous, because the rewritten arm
  A DELETEs the row and arm K UPSERTs it FALSE — each dormant arm now builds the
  state it measures. The pre-164.7 arm A read a SESSION setting it never set,
  which is why the inherited comment called this an anti-vacuity guard; that was
  true of the old arm and is false of the new one.

The precondition was NOT deleted, because leg (a) shows it firing on a real
condition worth firing on: a committed TRUE row means an operator has the ledger
refresh LIVE, and this file writes that row inside its transaction. Its comment
and its RAISE message were rewritten to say that instead of the retired claim
(commit `04c33bb6`).

